1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,950
Behind me, an immense tea plantation.

2
00:00:03,050 --> 00:00:04,600
Leased for 3,000 rupees.

3
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:07,450
These hills have a story that begins with
a lease.

4
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:09,550
Tea leaves are still picked by hand.

5
00:00:09,580 --> 00:00:11,750
I'll find out the craft behind this work.

6
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:15,500
And the story behind picking the tea we
drink every day.

7
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:18,950
The world, told by those who live it.

8
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:20,950
This is Voci Vere.

9
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:25,900
I'm in Munnar, Kerala, over 1,500 metres
up.

10
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:32,650
In 1877 a raja of the Poonjar family
leases these hills to John Daniel Munro,

11
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:35,150
a Briton working for the kingdom of
Travancore.

12
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:38,250
About 136,000 acres, for 3,000 rupees a
year.

13
00:00:38,350 --> 00:00:40,650
At first they try coffee, cardamom,
cinchona.

14
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:43,950
The first tea is planted in 1880, on about
fifty acres.

15
00:00:44,450 --> 00:00:47,600
Sadish is from here. He takes us where
local people pick the tea.

16
00:00:47,850 --> 00:00:49,400
Not the view: the work.

17
00:00:49,650 --> 00:00:51,100
This is the tea plantation.

18
00:00:51,250 --> 00:00:55,750
All the mountains you see, everywhere, are
tea.

19
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:57,800
Greenery. Green blankets.

20
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,800
They cover all the mountains.

21
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:05,500
In Munnar almost everyone works in tea.

22
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:10,380
Since before I was born, my father and
mother

23
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:15,700
worked in tea: for the government, for the
companies, on the plantations.

24
00:01:16,350 --> 00:01:19,400
A tea plant lives more than a hundred
years.

25
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:22,200
This plant is more than a hundred years
old.

26
00:01:22,350 --> 00:01:23,650
Rainwater only.

27
00:01:23,750 --> 00:01:29,200
Of course it's all organic: no chemicals,
no pesticides, only rain.

28
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:36,900
These leaves are picked every ten, fifteen
days.

29
00:01:37,100 --> 00:01:42,950
This is the plucking method: two, three
leaves.

30
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:46,400
Two, three leaves: those are the good
quality ones.

31
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:49,600
These, the good ones, go to the factory.

32
00:01:49,850 --> 00:01:54,200
These dark, black ones are bad leaves.
They're not used.

33
00:01:54,950 --> 00:01:56,400
Who picks?

34
00:01:56,550 --> 00:01:59,100
Everyone: women and men.

35
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:05,100
Today I pick here, tomorrow I go to
another hill, another place.

36
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:08,400
Then next week, maybe next month, we come
back round.

37
00:02:08,850 --> 00:02:11,600
Today here, next month here again.

38
00:02:11,850 --> 00:02:15,250
There are different tea leaves, as I
showed.

39
00:02:15,450 --> 00:02:19,150
These smaller ones are used for green tea:
they're more prized.

40
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:23,400
And they don't pick them all: these with
holes, these damaged ones,

41
00:02:23,450 --> 00:02:25,050
they don't use them at all, they don't
pick them.

42
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:27,400
In general, there's a process behind it.

43
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:31,000
Working hours are from 8 in the morning to
12.

44
00:02:31,020 --> 00:02:32,850
An hour, an hour and a half for lunch,

45
00:02:32,870 --> 00:02:38,550
then they start again in the afternoon, from half
past one or two, until about 5, he told me.

46
00:02:38,950 --> 00:02:42,500
Normally, per day, one person picks

47
00:02:43,250 --> 00:02:48,400
twenty-six to twenty-nine kilos a day.

48
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:50,400
On average.

49
00:02:51,350 --> 00:02:53,950
And people who pick more?

50
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:55,400
Extra money.

51
00:02:56,700 --> 00:02:58,100
Where do the leaves go?

52
00:02:58,350 --> 00:03:01,100
To the tea factory.

53
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:05,700
There are many factories here in Munnar.

54
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:14,400
There are three, four nearby: twenty,
twenty-five, thirty kilometres away.

55
00:03:14,650 --> 00:03:16,950
In India, tea is mostly picked by women.

56
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:19,300
The leaves are plucked by hand, row after
row.

57
00:03:19,850 --> 00:03:24,950
Then the leaves go to the factory. With
the orthodox method they're withered, rolled, oxidised and dried.

58
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:27,850
Withering takes eighteen to twenty hours
on average.

59
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:30,500
Do you know the history?

60
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:32,500
1877.

61
00:03:32,650 --> 00:03:33,700
He was an Englishman.

62
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:41,300
In Munnar, all around, north, south, east,
west, for forty kilometres,

63
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:44,400
tea plantations everywhere.

64
00:03:44,550 --> 00:03:52,000
All these mountains are called the Kannan
Devan Hills.

65
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:58,450
In 1895 a Scottish company from Glasgow,
Finlay Muir, arrives and buys thirty-three plantations.

66
00:03:58,550 --> 00:04:02,550
Two years later the Kannan Devan Hills
Produce Company is founded.

67
00:04:02,750 --> 00:04:05,800
From 1901 the forest is cut down on a
large scale.

68
00:04:05,850 --> 00:04:09,350
By 1911 tea covers about 6,500 hectares.

69
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:11,950
Tamil workers are brought in to work here.

70
00:04:12,050 --> 00:04:15,600
Before them, these hills are home to the
Muthuvan and the Malayarayan.

71
00:04:16,550 --> 00:04:23,600
How is the relationship between this big
company and the people who live here?

72
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:27,300
Have they been treated well?

73
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:35,700
A very good relationship: guidance, help,
home, medical care, transport.

74
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:38,200
The company gives everything for free.

75
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:42,350
In 1971 Kerala passes a law on the land of
these hills.

76
00:04:42,450 --> 00:04:46,450
Since 2005 most of the company has
belonged to its employees, about thirteen thousand.

77
00:04:46,550 --> 00:04:50,250
According to Tata, it's the first plantation
company in India to work this way.

78
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:52,800
Centuries of work, in which...

79
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,700
Indian workers, women and men, as Sadish
told us,

80
00:04:57,850 --> 00:05:02,200
are treated well, with respect for their
tradition:

81
00:05:02,250 --> 00:05:05,000
for them it becomes part of the culture.

82
00:05:05,550 --> 00:05:09,420
In 2015 thousands of women pickers block
Munnar for nine days,

83
00:05:09,450 --> 00:05:10,950
without unions or parties.

84
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,250
They call themselves Pombilai Orumai:
women's unity.

85
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:17,350
They ask for higher wages. They win the
20% bonus.

86
00:05:17,550 --> 00:05:24,450
Do you know that India is the second
largest tea producer in the world, after China?

87
00:05:24,550 --> 00:05:26,950
Yes, India is second.

88
00:05:27,100 --> 00:05:29,900
There's a very deep culture here.

89
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,300
The best production in India. Good
quality.

90
00:05:34,750 --> 00:05:39,750
The company of these hills produced about twenty-five
million kilos a year, according to 2019 figures,

91
00:05:39,770 --> 00:05:41,700
and sells most of it to other brands.

92
00:05:43,700 --> 00:05:47,600
And now it's time to try this traditional
tea, right here.

93
00:05:48,300 --> 00:05:50,900
He is the chai master.

94
00:05:50,950 --> 00:05:52,900
I serve.

95
00:05:53,900 --> 00:05:56,100
Finally, let's taste this tea.

96
00:05:56,350 --> 00:06:01,900
I tried to ask the tea master, that's what
they call him, a few questions, and he refused.

97
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:04,900
Maybe this is the essence of India.

98
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:09,450
Doing things without needing anyone's praise,
doing them because you love doing them.

99
00:06:09,950 --> 00:06:14,400
Next time we drink a cup of tea, it's
worth remembering where it comes from. And who picks it.

100
00:06:14,650 --> 00:06:17,750
The Voci Vere journey, to give a voice to
those who need it most, goes on.

101
00:06:17,950 --> 00:06:20,300
Follow me to discover the next stories.
